  ],
  "entries": [
    {"key": "PIO.01.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer: a word for those who go first. And the name was no accident."},
    {"key": "PIO.01.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "March 1972. No spacecraft had ever traveled beyond Mars. This probe, about the size of a car, was about to try."},
    {"key": "PIO.01.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Eleven hours after liftoff, it passed the Moon."},
    {"key": "PIO.01.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It spins slowly. The spin keeps it steady and its antenna pointed at Earth."},
    {"key": "PIO.01.05", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Far from the Sun, sunlight is too weak. So Pioneer made its own power with RTG generators."},
    {"key": "PIO.01.06", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "No human-made object had ever moved this fast."},
    {"key": "PIO.02.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Mars was behind it. Ahead: the asteroid belt, a place no spacecraft had ever crossed."},
    {"key": "PIO.02.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Nobody knew how many rocks were out there. Nobody knew if a spacecraft could get through."},
    {"key": "PIO.02.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Some scientists feared that one collision could end the mission."},
    {"key": "PIO.02.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Then, a surprise: the rocks were far apart. Pioneer’s route passed millions of kilometers from the nearest known asteroid."},
    {"key": "PIO.02.05", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Only fine dust brushed the probe."},
    {"key": "PIO.02.06", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Seven months later, Pioneer came out the other side, unharmed. The belt could be crossed."},
    {"key": "PIO.02.07", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "So the days were counted: a week, a month, then months flying by faster and faster."},
    {"key": "PIO.03.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Next came the biggest prize: Jupiter, the largest planet. By November 1973, it was in sight."},
    {"key": "PIO.03.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Jupiter is so big that eleven Earths could fit side by side across it."},
    {"key": "PIO.03.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer started taking pictures from 25 million kilometers away."},
    {"key": "PIO.03.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "But a command and its reply could take up to an hour and a half."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer didn’t have a camera like the one in your phone."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Each time it spun, it saw one thin strip of Jupiter, in just two colors: red and blue."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It measured red and blue together, but a single picture was full of noise."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Add many pictures together, and the noise fades and Jupiter appears."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.05", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer measured red and blue. Earth had to work out the third color."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.06", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer never saw green. Computers on Earth estimated it, and Jupiter appeared in color."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.07", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "A real photo from 1973: Jupiter, as Pioneer 10 saw it."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.08", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It was the first spacecraft to take close-up pictures of Jupiter."},
    {"key": "PIO.04.09", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "They were sharper than anything telescopes on Earth could show."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Closer in, it passed Jupiter’s big moons and took the first close-ups of Ganymede, the largest moon in the Solar System."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Jupiter is surrounded by radiation thousands of times stronger than Earth’s."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer took in over a thousand times the radiation dose that would kill a person."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Closest approach: Pioneer 10 became the first spacecraft to fly past Jupiter."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.05", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It passed 130,000 kilometers above the clouds, three times closer than the Moon is to Earth."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.06", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Jupiter’s gravity bent its path and gave it the speed to travel beyond the planets."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.07", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Jupiter’s radiation turned out stronger than expected, and that changed Voyager’s design."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.08", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It also photographed Callisto and Europa, but only at low resolution."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.09", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Jupiter is also moving around the Sun, and Pioneer borrowed a bit of that speed, like hitching a ride."},
    {"key": "PIO.09.10", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It also took a picture of Io, but the radiation destroyed it."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Jupiter fell behind. There was no way back: Pioneer was headed for the stars."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.01b", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It carried a message for anyone who might find it: a golden plaque with no words."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.01c", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Just pictures and symbols, showing who we are and where we came from."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Hydrogen, the Universe’s most common atom, is our yardstick."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Next, a map of pulsars, stars that blink, showing where the Sun is."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Then our Solar System, showing Pioneer leaving the third planet, Earth."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.05", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Beside it, an outline of Pioneer itself, for scale."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.06", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "And two figures. One of them is waving hello."},
    {"key": "PIO.05.07", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "A gold-colored aluminum plaque: a letter for a meeting that may never happen."},
    {"key": "PIO.06.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer was built to last 21 months. It just kept going."},
    {"key": "PIO.06.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "June 13, 1983: Pioneer passed Neptune’s orbit, with all its instruments still working normally."},
    {"key": "PIO.06.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "By 1989, its power was fading. The instruments took turns, and the command computer was shut off."},
    {"key": "PIO.06.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "In 1998, Voyager 1 passed Pioneer to become the most distant human-made object."},
    {"key": "PIO.06.05", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer had shown the way. Now Earth had to listen from farther and farther away."},
    {"key": "PIO.06.06", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "To save power, the camera that photographed Jupiter was turned off, then the radiation detector."},
    {"key": "PIO.07.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Its signal was now weaker than a whisper. Could Earth still hear it?"},
    {"key": "PIO.07.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Yes. Thirty years later, Pioneer was still answering."},
    {"key": "PIO.07.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "April 27, 2002: the last data arrived."},
    {"key": "PIO.07.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "January 23, 2003: one last signal, from 12 billion kilometers away, took over eleven hours to reach us."},
    {"key": "PIO.07.05", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "February 7, 2003: Earth listened again and heard nothing."},
    {"key": "PIO.07.06", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Its power had finally run out, and Pioneer 10 went silent. But it didn’t stop."},
    {"key": "PIO.08.01", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "It’s still traveling, toward the red star Aldebaran, a trip of about two million years."},
    {"key": "PIO.08.02", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Built to last 21 months, it worked for more than 30 years."},
    {"key": "PIO.08.03", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "The name was no accident. Look at what it did first."},
    {"key": "PIO.08.04", "group": "narrative", "status": "derived", "text": "Pioneer means going first. Every great journey starts with someone willing to go first. Pioneer 10 went first, for all of us."},

